# Explicit assembled-host verification

This required release profile runs seven complete SQLite host cases and seven deployment scenarios. It is deliberately outside unconditional Node/Vitest discovery. The normal inventory includes the fast qualification controls; it does not silently run native hosts or count this profile as passed.

The command is `node tools/assembled-host-profile.mjs`. It requires `--runtime-root`, `--input-manifest`, `--build-manifest` and a new `--out` directory below this checkout's `.runtime`. `--check` performs read-only prerequisite checks and returns READY or INCOMPLETE. Only a completed native run returns PASS. Missing prerequisites return exit 3; failures return exit 1. No test is skipped to produce either result.

For an existing successful engine build, `--prepare --runtime-root ROOT --build-manifest BUILD --out NEW_MANIFEST_FILE` records the current runtime-source inventory. Preparation hashes existing files; it does not compile, export source, install dependencies or select an arbitrary `dist`. It then qualifies the existing build. A clean current checkout must match its build's exact source commit and raw committed bytes. A source snapshot must match its HANDOVER manifest, complete runtime closure, actual changed-file digest and sourceSnapshot identity from the existing sourceProvenance189 producer. A legacy assembled native build must bind the exact source manifest and record successful native status, null signal/error and settled hidden compiler. Missing or inconsistent evidence remains INCOMPLETE.

Run with the qualified manifest and build receipt. Admission checks every source module, schema, vendor and fixture input; the actual dist digest; the actual SQLite native addon/package; the synthetic SQLite fixture; and all control/checker/observer bytes. The source pin comes from that manifest, never the old a119 pin. Historical test preimages in older assembly manifests are retained as historical inputs; this profile separately pins its explicit controls.

Both controls copy only runtime files and synthetic fixture data into owned output directories. They resolve the same existing dependencies as this checkout. The source/runtime snapshot itself stays read-only, so the original review fixture can remain in recoverable custody. No full source export or dependency junction is required. Hosts use owned loopback ports; the deployed service on 3142 is refused. Network guards, response/status/token/cookie/no-write assertions, drain behavior, native failures and database-close observations remain the original contracts.

PASS requires assertion-level host evidence, all seven deployment scenarios, stable source/runtime/control inputs, settled recorded process IDs, and verified cleanup of owned runtime/database copies. Cleanup uses the existing scratch ownership/removal module. An incomplete failure retains its unknown scratch rather than removing evidence without process clearance. Raw logs and case receipts remain in the explicit output. Physical allocation is not inferred from logical file lengths.

The release battery accepts `--assembled-host-report OUTPUT/summary.json`. It rechecks the source pin, current qualification, both native exits, exact scenario counts and cleanup. Missing, stale, foreign-source or incomplete profile evidence prevents release-battery PASS. This is an additional explicit obligation; it does not establish whole-port, hosted, native Ruby, Bun, D1, Linux or installed acceptance.

The immutable original controls and their earlier failure receipts remain under the accepted 047/dc2 evidence packets. Source-preservation tests compare all original scenario bodies after setup-path normalization. Profile preparation changes no accepted product hunks.
